-- title:
--   Representability and projectivity of the Hilbert functor Hilb^P_{Pⁿ}
-- statement:
--   Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $P \in \mathbb{Q}[t]$, and assume that $P$ is realised as an eventual Hilbert function: there are a field $K$, an ideal $I \subseteq K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ closed under taking homogeneous components (every homogeneous component of every element of $I$ lies in $I$), and a bound $d_1$ such that for all $d \ge d_1$ the $K$-dimension of `piece I d`, the space of degree-$d$ homogeneous forms modulo those in $I$, equals $P(d)$. The assertion is that there is $D_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $m \ge D_0$, writing $h =$ `hilbertFunctionOf n P m` for the function $d \mapsto \binom{n+d}{n}$ for $d < m$ and $d \mapsto \max(\lfloor P(d)\rfloor, 0)$ for $d \ge m$, the following hold. Here, for a commutative ring $A$, `Point A n h` is the type of ideals $I \subseteq A[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ closed under homogeneous components such that each `piece I d` is a finite projective $A$-module whose rank at every stalk of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ equals $h(d)$. First, base change: for every ring homomorphism $\varphi : A \to B$ between commutative rings and every $x \in$ `Point A n h` there is $y \in$ `Point B n h` with $y.I$ the ideal generated by the image of $x.I$ under the coefficientwise map $\varphi$. Secondly, there exist a scheme $\mathrm{Hilb}$ and bijections `Point A n h` $\simeq (\operatorname{Spec} A \to \mathrm{Hilb})$, one for each commutative ring $A$, compatible with base change in the sense that whenever $y.I$ is the extension of $x.I$ along $\varphi$, the morphism attached to $y$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi$ followed by the morphism attached to $x$. Thirdly, there are $N \in \mathbb{N}$ and a closed immersion $\mathrm{Hilb} \to \operatorname{Proj}\mathbb{Z}[x_0,\dots,x_N]$.
--
--   This is the existence of the Hilbert scheme parametrising homogeneous ideals in $n+1$ variables with prescribed truncated Hilbert function, together with its projectivity over $\mathbb{Z}$, in the ideal-theoretic formulation; the bound $D_0$ plays the role of the Gotzmann number of $P$. It is used to obtain the properness and local finite presentation of the resulting scheme.
